-- title:
--   A ball about an axis point lies inside the cone
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real inner product space, let $v \in E$ satisfy $\|v\| = 1$, and let $\vartheta$ satisfy $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$. Let $t > 0$, so that $t\,v$ is a point at distance $t$ from the origin along the axis. If $u \in E$ satisfies
--   $$\|\,u - t\,v\,\| \;<\; t \sin\vartheta ,$$
--   then $u \in \mathcal{C}(v,\vartheta)$, the open cone with axis $v$ and half-angle $\vartheta$.
--
--   Equivalently: the open ball of radius $t \sin \vartheta$ centred at the axis point $t v$ is contained in the cone — the radius $t\sin\vartheta$ being exactly the distance from $tv$ to the boundary of the cone. This is the quantitative geometric fact that converts an angular condition into a metric one, allowing a whole ball of points to be certified as lying in a cone from a single computation, and it is the form in which cone membership is checked whenever one needs a set of positive measure rather than a single point.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The bound is strict, matching the openness of the cone; the hypothesis $t > 0$ is essential, since for $t \le 0$ the point $tv$ does not lie in the cone at all.
-- source:
